# Mutation cases for "a transition with no target ends a microstep, not the
# macrostep" on the two engines a cargo round cannot see — the C++ AOT engine
# header and the C++ Interpreter.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/targetless_transition_completes_macrostep_ctest.cases`.
#
# Same axis as `targetless_transition_completes_macrostep.cases`, which holds
# the Kotlin code generator's half. W3C SCXML Appendix D's main event loop
# returns to `selectEventlessTransitions()` after every microstep and drains
# the internal queue in the same inner loop, without asking whether the
# microstep moved the machine — §scxml-3.13 defines a transition with no
# `target` as one that exits and enters nothing and runs its content in place.
#
# Measured 2026-08-20, these two got it wrong in two different places, which is
# why they are worth their own round:
#
#   - both ended the macrostep at the transition itself, so a chain its content
#     enabled was never walked, and on the AOT engine the caller's queue drain
#     was skipped too, so a `<raise>` from a targetless transition stayed
#     queued past the host call;
#   - the AOT engine also dropped a targetless transition SELECTED BY THE
#     DRAIN: its loop continued only while the configuration kept changing, so
#     it took the transition, ran none of its content, and left.
#
# The C11, Go, Python and Rust engines answered the fixture correctly on first
# measurement. Their channels assert it; only the Rust one is mutated, in
# `targetless_transition_completes_macrostep_rust.cases`.
#
# Every ctest entry carries a TIMEOUT, so a mutant that never ends comes back
# as an ordinary red rather than a hung round.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_ctest --test-dir build -R '^(IntegrationTests|c11_integration_targetless_transition_completes_macrostep)$'
mutation_targets sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h sce/src/runtime/StateMachine.cpp
mutation_oracles tests/integration/TargetlessTransitionCompletesMacrostepAotTest.cpp tests/integration/TargetlessTransitionCompletesMacrostepTest.cpp

# ── C++ AOT: the chain the transition opened ───────────────────────
#
# The shipped early return. The transition's content runs, and whatever it
# enabled is left for nobody: the host is handed a configuration §scxml-3.13
# calls unstable, with `getCurrentState()` naming a state the document was
# supposed to have left.

mutation_case "the aot engine returns from a targetless transition without walking the chain" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """        // content opens a chain as surely as one that entered a state.
        postTransition();
        checkEventlessTransitions();""",
     """        // content opens a chain as surely as one that entered a state.
        if (std::all_of(taken.begin(), taken.end(), [](const TransitionInfo &t) { return t.isTargetless(); })) {
            return EventOutcome{/*selected=*/true, /*configurationChanged=*/false};
        }
        postTransition();
        checkEventlessTransitions();""")
PY

# ── C++ AOT: the queue the transition filled ───────────────────────
#
# The other side of the same inner loop, and the reason the two are separate
# cases: `postTransition` is the caller's drain and `checkEventlessTransitions`
# is the eventless half, so an engine can lose one and keep the other. On the
# direct `processEvent` path this one is the whole of the internal queue, and
# without it a `<raise>` from a targetless transition is answered on some later
# host call, or never.

mutation_case "the aot engine skips the queue drain after a targetless transition" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """        // content opens a chain as surely as one that entered a state.
        postTransition();
        checkEventlessTransitions();""",
     """        // content opens a chain as surely as one that entered a state.
        if (std::any_of(taken.begin(), taken.end(), [](const TransitionInfo &t) { return !t.isTargetless(); })) {
            postTransition();
        }
        checkEventlessTransitions();""")
PY

# ── C++ AOT: the targetless link inside the chain ──────────────────
#
# The drain selected the transition and then dropped it, because the loop
# continued only on a changed configuration. The content never ran, so the
# chain ended one microstep early with the machine resting where that link
# began — `chained == 1, polished == 0` in the fixture's terms. The mutation
# restores exactly that: a microstep of targetless transitions is counted and
# left untaken.

mutation_case "the aot drain selects a targetless microstep and runs none of it" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """            // content in place, and the chain it opens is walked like any other.
            microstep(enabled);""",
     """            // content in place, and the chain it opens is walked like any other.
            if (std::all_of(enabled.begin(), enabled.end(), [](const TransitionInfo &t) { return t.isTargetless(); })) {
                break;
            }
            microstep(enabled);""")
PY

# ── C++ AOT: the self transition that still has work ───────────────
#
# A self transition with a target ends where it started too, and the same
# "configuration did not change" test used to drop its exit and entry on the
# floor. The fixture's `recycle` outcome is what holds it: an eventless self
# transition, guarded so the chain is finite, whose `<onentry>` counts the
# entries. A mutant that drops the exit and entry leaves `entries == 1`.
#
# That outcome exists because this case SURVIVED without it — the first draft
# of this file said in as many words that the fixture could not separate the
# two, which is a case with no witness rather than a case. Writing the witness
# was the repair. The mutation restores the "configuration did not change"
# shortcut in the one microstep every transition now takes: a transition that
# names its own source runs its content and neither exits nor enters.

mutation_case "the aot drain drops the exit and entry of an eventless self transition" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h",
     """    void microstep(const std::vector<TransitionInfo> &transitions) {""",
     """    void microstep(const std::vector<TransitionInfo> &transitions) {
        MicrostepHost shortcut{*this};
        if (std::all_of(transitions.begin(), transitions.end(), [&shortcut](const TransitionInfo &t) {
                const std::vector<State> targets = SCE::Core::MicrostepAlgorithms::effectiveTargets(shortcut, t);
                return targets.size() == 1 && targets.front() == t.source;
            })) {
            SCE::Core::MicrostepAlgorithms::executeTransitionContent(shortcut, transitions);
            return;
        }""")
PY

# ── C++ Interpreter: the chain the transition opened ───────────────
#
# The same defect in the other engine, in the other place. The main event loop
# runs after the event's microstep whatever that microstep did; the mutation
# puts back the shortcut the defect was — a transition that ended where it
# started, a targetless one above all, reads as having moved nothing, and the
# chain its content opened is never walked.

mutation_case "the interpreter returns from a targetless transition without walking the chain" <<'PY'
edit("sce/src/runtime/StateMachine.cpp",
     """    const TransitionResult result = processTakenEvent(event, fromExternalQueue);
    runMainEventLoop();""",
     """    const TransitionResult result = processTakenEvent(event, fromExternalQueue);
    if (result.toState != result.fromState) {
        runMainEventLoop();
    }""")
PY

# ── C++ Interpreter: the chain walked to its end ───────────────────
#
# A case once stood absent here, deliberately: replacing the `while` around the
# old `checkEventlessTransitions()` with a single call SURVIVED a full round,
# because that function walked the chain itself, re-entering through state
# entry, so one call and a loop were the same behaviour. The note said the loop
# stayed because it is "what stays correct if that recursion is ever unwound".
# It has been: the main event loop now takes eventless microsteps one after
# another and nothing re-enters it, so taking one and stopping is a decision
# of its own, and gets its case. The fixture's `recycle` outcome counts the
# entries of a chain several microsteps long.

mutation_case "the interpreter takes one eventless microstep and stops" <<'PY'
edit("sce/src/runtime/StateMachine.cpp",
     """                ++macrostepMicrostepsTaken_;
                takeMicrostep(enabled, std::string{});
                continue;""",
     """                ++macrostepMicrostepsTaken_;
                takeMicrostep(enabled, std::string{});
                break;""")
PY
